  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 PM EDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nEarly afternoon GOES satellite imagery reveals mostly clear skies \nacross Upper Michigan save for a bubbling cu field in the western \ninterior. Very light radar returns <25 dbz have been observed in the \nlast hour where maybe some sprinkles or very light rain may be \nreaching the surface, but the majority of CAMs haven't latched onto \nthe idea of any precipitation occuring out that way, so added some \n15% PoPs for a very brief shower through the evening. Given ongoing \ndry air advection, suspecting the area will remain precip free. A \nfew light rain showers may be seen in the Keweenaw this \nafternoon/evening as showers currently crossing Isle Royale journey \nsoutheast across the lake. Otherwise with increasing high pressure \nand dry air in place, temperatures tonight could be rather chilly \nespecially for the interior. Opting to continue the trend of erring \non the lower side of guidance for overnight lows- 50s lakeshores and \n40s elsewhere with pockets of upper 30s in the typical cool spots of \nthe interior. Patchy fog and or developing stratus may keep some \nlocations warmer than expected.\n\nPleasant weather sticks around through Friday under the influence of \nrising heights aloft and surface high pressure meandering through \nthe Great Lakes. A warmer and more active pattern shapes up this \nweekend and into the early parts of next week as height rises \nflatten out to more zonal flow aloft, allowing additional \nshortwaves to work through the area. Southerly return flow ahead of \nsaid shortwaves aims to usher in warmer (5-10 degree above normal \ndaytime highs, 5-15 above normal lows) and more humid airmasses. The \nnext best shot at widespread precip returns this Saturday into \nSunday with NBM probability of at least 0.1\" pegged between 50-75% \nfor much of the UP. That said, precip amounts will largely depend on \nthunderstorm coverage. Hopefully we can see enough rainfall in areas \nnow experiencing moderate to extreme drought across the southern and \nwestern UP.",
